WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.091
The highest-value hour in the project.

2
00:00:02.371 --> 00:00:06.851
Most teams, asked to “add evals,” start by choosing metrics or a tool.

3
00:00:07.131 --> 00:00:21.253
Husain and Shankar’s evals FAQ argues for the opposite order, calling error analysis “the most important activity in evals”: before you decide what to measure, read what your system actually does and find out how it fails.

4
00:00:21.533 --> 00:00:24.456
The reason is that you can’t measure what you haven’t named.

5
00:00:24.736 --> 00:00:29.301
A support bot’s real problems are rarely “hallucination” in the abstract.

6
00:00:29.581 --> 00:00:37.197
They are things like “tells customers about integrations we don’t have” or “tries to fix account lockouts instead of handing off.

7
00:00:37.477 --> 00:00:40.997
” Those are product-specific, and you only find them by reading.

8
00:00:41.277 --> 00:00:53.992
Husain’s field guide gives a striking example: in one client’s assistant, error analysis showed that three issues accounted for over 60 percent of all problems, which told the team where to spend its time.

9
00:00:54.272 --> 00:00:58.368
The unit you read is a trace: the complete record of one interaction.

10
00:00:58.648 --> 00:01:05.453
For a chat assistant that is the user message, what was retrieved, any tool calls and their results, and the reply.

11
00:01:05.733 --> 00:01:07.483
For an agent it is every step.

12
00:01:07.763 --> 00:01:19.027
Anthropic’s evals post makes the same point from the other direction: read many transcripts, because a score alone can’t tell you whether a failure is the system’s fault or the grader’s.

13
00:01:19.307 --> 00:01:26.411
This lesson walks through the method: sample traces, write open notes, group them into a taxonomy, count, and decide.

14
00:01:26.691 --> 00:01:31.064
Then the lab has you do it on 36 traces from a fictional support bot.
